.TH NAZUKE 1 "2026-11-04" "nazuke" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
nazuke \- give long names short, readable, unique ones, 8.3 by default
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B nazuke
.br
.B nazuke name
.RI [ options ] " " text ...
.br
.B nazuke rename
.RI [ options ] " " [ folder ]
.br
.B nazuke doctor
.br
.B nazuke version
.br
.B nazuke help
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B nazuke
turns a long name, such as a release name or a document title, into a short
one that a person can still read: eight capital letters and digits by
default, the base of an 8.3 file name. It keeps whole words where it can,
keeps every digit, drops bracketed tags such as
.IR (USA) ,
articles and small words such as
.I of
and
.IR the ,
and shortens a subtitle to its initials. A disc, disk, CD, side, tape or part
number inside a tag, such as
.IR "(Disc 2)" ,
stays. When the digits alone leave no room, a run of five or more digits is
dropped first and then every digit; a name of digits only keeps its first
ones. Accented and other Latin letters become plain ones, so
.I Stra\(sse
becomes
.BR STRASSE .
.PP
Several forms of each name are scored, and the best is kept: whole words and
the last word kept intact score high, truncated words and words cut to one
letter score low, and dropping a word costs much more than dropping a generic
one from the word pack.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.BI name " text ..."
Print the short name of each text, one per line, in order.
.TP
.BI rename " [folder]"
Give every file at the top of the folder, the current one when none is given,
a short name that no other entry of the folder holds in any case. A file whose
name is already short, capitals and digits within
.BR \-\-max ,
keeps it, so a second run changes nothing. The extension after the last dot,
one to eight letters and digits with at least one letter, is kept in
capitals, so each name is a valid 8.3 name on FAT12 and FAT16, and shortened
to
.B \-\-ext
characters, using
.I jpg
for
.IR jpeg ,
.I htm
for
.IR html ,
.I unf
for
.I unif
and a few others. No new name is a device name such as
.B CON
or
.BR LPT1 .
Two names that collide take the region letter of their tag, such as
.B U
for
.IR (USA) ,
when the word pack has one, and a number otherwise, as long as the number
fits. When three or more files share the part of their name before
.BR " - " ,
that part is dropped from each of them, so the names come from what tells the
files apart. Subfolders, hidden files
and special files keep their names; symbolic links are renamed themselves.
Each file is moved to a hidden temporary name first, then to its new name, so
an interrupt puts back every name the run changed. Where gum draws, the plan
is shown and confirmed before anything moves.
.TP
.B doctor
List the tools nazuke needs, with the flavor and version of each. Exits
non-zero only when one is missing.
.TP
.B version
Print the version and the platform.
.TP
.B help
Print the usage page.
.PP
With no command on a terminal, nazuke asks which job to do.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-max " n"
The longest name, 4 to 255. The default is 8.
.TP
.BI \-\-ext " n"
For rename, the longest extension, 1 to 255. The default is 3.
.TP
.BI \-\-words " pack"
.B generic
for any name,
.B games
for release names, which also drops generic title words such as Legend and
Super and reads region tags, or the path of a word pack of your own. The
default is
.BR generic .
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
For rename, list every rename and change nothing.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Print the result as one JSON object on stdout. For name: the settings and each
text with its name. For rename: the folder, the settings, the counts of files
found, renamed, kept and failed, and each rename.
.TP
.BI \-\-theme " name"
Colours of the terminal interface:
.BR kodougu ,
.BR catppuccin\-mocha ,
.BR catppuccin\-latte ,
.BR dracula ,
.BR nord ,
.BR gruvbox ,
.B tokyo\-night
or
.BR mono .
The default is
.BR kodougu .
.PP
.B \-\-
ends the options, so a text or a folder may start with a dash.
.SH WORD PACKS
A word pack is a text file of lines, each a key followed by words in capitals:
.TP
.BI fallback " WORD"
The name of a text with nothing to keep.
.TP
.BI stopwords " WORD ..."
Small words that are dropped.
.TP
.BI articles " WORD ..."
Articles dropped at the start, or moved from the end as in
.IR "Legend of Zelda, The" .
.TP
.BI droppable " WORD ..."
Generic words that are cheap to drop.
.TP
.BI region " WORD LETTER"
A tag word and the letter that stands for it in a collision.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B NAZUKE_MAX
The longest name when
.B \-\-max
is not given.
.TP
.B NAZUKE_EXT
The longest extension when
.B \-\-ext
is not given.
.TP
.B NAZUKE_WORDS
The word pack when
.B \-\-words
is not given.
.TP
.B NAZUKE_THEME
The theme when
.B \-\-theme
is not given.
.TP
.B KODOUGU_THEME
The theme of every kodougu tool, when neither
.B \-\-theme
nor
.B NAZUKE_THEME
is set.
.TP
.B KODOUGU_ACCENT
A colour such as #CBA6F7 that replaces the accent of the theme.
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
Turns colour off in every theme.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success, including a rename run with nothing to rename. 1 when a file
could not be renamed, no short name was left for one, or the arguments are
wrong. 130 after an interrupt.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B nazuke name \(dqLegend of Zelda, The \- Ocarina of Time (USA)\(dq
Print LEZELOOT, or ZELDAOOT with
.BR "\-\-words games" .
.TP
.B nazuke rename \-\-words games \-\-dry\-run ~/roms
Show the short names a folder of games would get.
.TP
.B nazuke rename \-\-max 12 \-\-ext 4 .
Give the current folder names of up to twelve characters.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mv (1)
